---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { page, breadcrumb } from '../lib/seo';
const schema = [page('WebPage', { site:Astro.site, path:'/concepts', name:'Local AI concepts in plain English', description:'The few concepts needed to read Ground Floor estimates and protocols.' }), breadcrumb(Astro.site, [{name:'Concepts',path:'/concepts'}])];
---
<Base title="Local AI concepts in plain English" description="The few concepts needed to read Ground Floor estimates and protocols." appearance="light" schema={schema}>
  <header class="gf-page-hero"><div class="gf-shell"><p class="gf-eyebrow">Plain-English reference</p><h1 class="gf-display mt-4">Four ideas make the setup legible.</h1><p class="gf-lede">You do not need to become a model engineer. You do need to separate memory fit, context, output quality, and the complete system around the model.</p></div></header>
  <section class="gf-content"><div class="gf-shell"><div class="gf-task-list"><div class="gf-task"><div><strong>Model weights</strong><p>The model artifact must fit in available memory alongside the runtime, context, and operating system.</p></div></div><div class="gf-task"><div><strong>Quantization</strong><p>Reduced-precision weights can lower memory demand. The exact artifact still matters.</p></div></div><div class="gf-task"><div><strong>Context</strong><p>Instructions, source text, conversation history, and generated output all consume working memory.</p></div></div><div class="gf-task"><div><strong>The complete data path</strong><p>A model can run on-device while sync, backups, telemetry, plugins, or transcription still use a network.</p></div></div></div><div class="gf-form mt-12"><h2 class="gf-h2">Fit is not quality.</h2><p class="gf-copy mt-4">A model artifact fitting in memory says nothing by itself about accuracy, speed, usefulness, security, or professional suitability. Those require separate observation.</p><a class="gf-link inline-flex mt-5" href="/playbook">See how evidence is earned →</a></div></div></section>
</Base>
